WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.160
How can a whale with legs be older than a whale built for open water?

2
00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:10.480
Imagine a cliff that holds both kinds of fossil, then read its layers like a record

3
00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:11.480
of events.

4
00:00:14.433 --> 00:00:19.913
A fossil is preserved evidence of past life, such as a bone, shell, tooth, or

5
00:00:19.913 --> 00:00:25.073
footprint. Watch sediment bury one fossil, settle, then bury another.

6
00:00:26.393 --> 00:00:28.893
The lower layer formed before the layer above it.

7
00:00:29.817 --> 00:00:31.717
That order is relative dating.

8
00:00:32.997 --> 00:00:36.497
It tells which layer is older, without giving an age in years.

9
00:00:37.797 --> 00:00:40.717
In an undisturbed stack, lower layers formed first.

10
00:00:41.957 --> 00:00:44.397
The time arrow points upward toward younger rock.

11
00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:49.920
The fossil record is every fossil found so far, arranged by age.

12
00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:55.240
It shows kinds of life appearing, changing, and often going extinct.

13
00:00:56.660 --> 00:00:59.240
One cliff holds only a small piece of that record.

14
00:01:00.283 --> 00:01:04.143
Now the cliff tilts, and one block turns almost upside down.

15
00:01:05.403 --> 00:01:07.523
Which fossil is older after the rocks move?

16
00:01:08.823 --> 00:01:11.783
Follow the matching layers, not simply the bottom edge.

17
00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:21.190
Tilting changes the angle, not the order of formation.

18
00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:26.310
Folding, flipping, or faults can move old rock above young rock.

19
00:01:27.710 --> 00:01:31.970
Geologists reconstruct the stack before using the lower-means-older rule.

20
00:01:32.983 --> 00:01:35.003
Relative order is not an age.

21
00:01:36.383 --> 00:01:39.823
Radioactive dating adds ages in years to certain minerals.

22
00:01:41.163 --> 00:01:44.723
Starting atoms change into product atoms at a steady rate.

23
00:01:45.903 --> 00:01:48.643
Their measured amounts estimate when the mineral formed.

24
00:01:49.717 --> 00:01:53.797
Watch two volcanic ash layers close around one fossil layer.

25
00:01:55.057 --> 00:01:58.557
The lower ash formed earlier, and the upper ash formed later.

26
00:01:59.857 --> 00:02:03.337
Their dates bracket the fossil, but neither gives its exact age.

27
00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:07.320
More often, no volcanic layer sits nearby.

28
00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:12.200
Scientists can match a fossil layer to dated rocks elsewhere.

29
00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:17.600
Index fossils help when their species lived during a known span of time.

30
00:02:18.633 --> 00:02:20.873
The cliff is not a complete diary.

31
00:02:22.253 --> 00:02:25.793
Most remains rot, are eaten, or never get buried quickly.

32
00:02:27.293 --> 00:02:30.273
Water and sediment favor some environments over others.

33
00:02:31.613 --> 00:02:34.573
The fossil record is a book with many missing pages.

34
00:02:35.717 --> 00:02:39.117
Even with gaps, an ordered sequence can test a history.

35
00:02:40.757 --> 00:02:42.917
Older whale fossils retain legs and feet.

36
00:02:44.497 --> 00:02:46.097
Later forms have smaller hind limbs.

37
00:02:47.437 --> 00:02:52.677
The sequence shows change, not that one named fossil was the direct ancestor of the

38
00:02:52.677 --> 00:02:53.677
next.

39
00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:55.990
One line of evidence can mislead.

40
00:02:57.710 --> 00:03:01.930
Rocks may be disturbed, dates may only bracket, and fossils may be missing.

41
00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:05.550
A different method can check the same history.

42
00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:11.630
Independent evidence that agrees strengthens an explanation of common descent.

43
00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:14.670
This film built a rock timeline.

44
00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:20.070
Bodies and inherited information can test the same branching history.
